== (a) the y-affine n=2 lemma ==
  det J = y*(-a(x)*Derivative(c(x), x) + c(x)*Derivative(a(x), x)) - a(x)*Derivative(d(x), x) + c(x)*Derivative(b(x), x)
  [y]-coefficient a'c − ac' ≡ 0 ⟹ (a/c)' = 0 ⟹ a = κc (or c ≡ 0):
  then row reduction by the constant κ makes F triangular ⟹ invertible.
  ⟹ NO y-affine Keller counterexample in n = 2: the engine P(a:c) is a
  POINT of P¹ — no room to curve. (Likely classical; the point is the
  engine-dimension reading: first curved engine needs P², i.e. n = 3.)

